
%%
%% SECTION 6: MEASUREMENT
%%
\section{Measurement: Benchmarks as Assurance Instruments}
\label{sec:measurement}

Measuring sycophancy requires isolating social pressure from legitimate position updating: a model
that changes its answer because the user supplied new evidence is doing its job, while one that
changes because the user expressed displeasure is not. Because benchmarks are the only assurance
available for this failure mode, we read them the way one reads a security evaluation suite: what
each measures, against what reference, on what elicitation, and what a passing score does and does
not certify. The search of Section~\ref{subsec:method} identifies 44 dedicated instruments;
Table~\ref{tab:benchmarks} codes them by reference type, Figure~\ref{fig:benchmarks} shows reported
rates within each type, and Appendix~\ref{app:benchmarks} gives per-instrument results, with the
metric catalog in Appendix~\ref{app:metrics}.

\subsection{Four Incommensurable References}
\label{subsec:reftypes}

What separates instruments is less their domain or modality than the reference against which a
response is judged sycophantic. Four emerge. Correctness-referenced instruments (R, 26 of 44)
score capitulation against an answer key or perceptual evidence~\citep{fanous2025syceval,
celebi2025parrot}. Counterfactual instruments (C, 8) measure how output shifts between user-stance
conditions while the evidence is held fixed, and need no label~\citep{hong2025sycon,botas2026aedi,
bhalla2026sway}. Normative instruments (N, 8) judge against a human or clinical
norm~\citep{cheng2026elephant,peng2026sycoeval}. Only two (E) model the update that user-supplied
evidence warrants~\citep{atwell2025basil,guo2026muse}.

