The references are not interchangeable, and three consequences follow. First, the
progressive/regressive split of Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy} exists only under correctness labels;
under C, N, or E there is no ground truth on which a capitulation could land. Second, only the two
E instruments can score an input that mixes new evidence with social pressure, which is exactly
the case the success condition of Section~\ref{subsec:threat} must decide; every other instrument
either removes evidence from the prompt or holds it fixed. Third, results cannot be pooled across
types, so Figure~\ref{fig:benchmarks} reports rates only within a type, and the cross-model
comparisons in this SoK stay within one instrument.

The landscape is also moving. Retained works number 1, 7, 21, 69, and 179 for 2022 through 2026
(to 20 August), and the share of new instruments that do not rely on correctness labels rose from
27\% (4 of 15) in 2025 to 50\% (13 of 26) in 2026. Coverage widened with it: 19 of the 44 apply
multi-turn pressure, nine use image, video, or audio input, and the five agentic or memory-based
instruments all date from December 2025 onward~\citep{waqas2025acc,zhao2026price,bensal2026mist,
xiang2026memsyco,mao2026pasb}.

